NoExecute:

Unlike NoSchedule—which only affects incoming pods—NoExecute actively clears out a node that is already running workloads.

How NoExecute Works

• Blocks New Pods: Any new pod without a matching toleration is rejected by the scheduler and will not be placed on the node.
• Evicts Existing Pods: Any pod already running on the node without a matching toleration is immediately terminated and evicted.
• Keeps Tolerating Pods: Pods that do have a matching toleration remain running on the node undisturbed.

Common Use Cases

• Automatic Node Failure Recovery: When a node crashes or loses network connection, the Kubernetes node controller automatically applies built-in NoExecute taints (such as node.kubernetes.io/not-ready or node.kubernetes.io/unreachable). By default, Kubernetes injects a 300-second tolerationSeconds into standard pods so they are automatically rescheduled to healthy nodes after 5 minutes of downtime.
• Emergency Node Draining: Administrators can manually apply a NoExecute taint to quickly clear non-essential workloads off a node undergoing urgent security patching or hardware maintenance.

